  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 143 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will shift west on Friday and upper-level \nwinds will continue to be northwesterly, shifting toward north-\nnorthwesterly Friday night. A weak cold front is expected to move in \nthroughout the day which will likely (barely) break the week-and-a-\nhalf streak of 100+ degree highs for most; highs are still forecast \nto be in the mid- to upper-90s across much of the area. A shortwave \ntrough is expected to move into the Panhandles later in the day. \nHowever, will have to watch what happens with the front; some \nguidance stalls it out in the southern Texas Panhandle, while others \npush the front well off to the south by the late afternoon. If the \nfront stalls out, thunderstorms will be possible again across the \nPanhandles but particularly where the front stalls. If the front \nkeeps moving south, then the northern Panhandles would most likely \nstay dry while the southern Texas Panhandle has a small window of \nopportunity for thunderstorms. \n\nThe weather for this weekend should be dry and seasonable(!!). For \nSaturday, we will likely be on the back side of a departing \nshortwave (negative vorticity advection), while the atmosphere will \nget gradually drier. Sunday, heights aloft will begin to rise as the \nridge over the Southwestern US begins to build back to the east and \nwe will start to see temperatures warm again from Sunday and into \nearly next week. However, with the center of the ridge likely to \nstay off to our west, we shouldn't see the same very hot \ntemperatures that we have been seeing over the past week and a half.\n\nVanden Bosch",
